"""Singapore flagship course content (10). Own wording."""
from flagship_nl_sg_au import M
from flagship_data_nl import K, SAFE, O4
C = []
def S(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, outs, jobs, mods, safety=SAFE):
    C.append(K(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, safety, outs, jobs, mods))
S('SG-01','Telecommunications Services','Plan, install-check and support a small home or office network service and measure service quality. You learn how a signal travels, how to survey a site, how to secure and test a network, how to find faults in a sensible order and how to explain fixes to non-technical users.',
 'Survey, set up and support one small network for a real user for 30 days and report uptime, speed, delay and faults, with a written support guide the user can follow without help.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. Router, network cables, free speed-test and ticket-log tools, a friendly site with a willing user, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Site survey table; working and secured network; fault log with causes and fixes; 30-day quality report with uptime percentage; safety and data checks signed; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain how a signal gets from provider to device.','Set up and test a small network.','Analyse speed, delay and faults.','Create a support guide for users.'),
 ['Network support technician','Service desk agent','Field installer assistant','Telecom operations assistant'],
 [M('How the network works','Understand',10,'A network is a chain of links from the provider to the exchange, the wider network and finally the device in your hand. Each link can be slow, late or unreliable, and a user feels each problem differently. Knowing the chain lets you point at the weak link instead of guessing.','The path runs from the line or wireless link into the router and then to each device, and you should be able to name every part on that path.|Speed means how much data arrives each second and is measured in megabits per second, written Mbps.|Delay means how long one small message takes to make a round trip, measured in milliseconds, written ms.|Reliability means how often the link stays up and keeps packets without loss, and a fast link that drops out is still a poor service.|A common mistake is to blame speed for every problem when the real cause is high delay or packet loss.','A home has a link of 100 Mbps down and 20 Mbps up. A video call needs about 3 Mbps each way, so speed is plenty. The call still stutters because delay is 150 ms, well above the 50 ms that feels smooth, so the weak link is delay and not speed.','Draw a labelled diagram of one real network, from the provider line to three devices, and write one sentence under each part saying what it does and what could go wrong there.','Your diagram names at least six parts in the correct order, and you correctly state which of speed, delay or reliability each part most affects.','What is the difference between speed and delay, and which one would make a video call stutter on a fast link?','Speed is the amount of data delivered each second, measured in Mbps, while delay is how long a message takes to travel there and back, measured in ms. A fast link with high delay makes a video call stutter because each piece of the conversation arrives late even though plenty of capacity exists.'),
  M('Survey the site','Apply',14,'A site survey finds where the signal is strong and weak before you place any equipment. Walls, floors, mirrors and other devices all weaken a wireless signal. A short, careful walk with a test tool saves hours of fixing later.','Walk every room with the same device and the same speed-test tool, and record the result at the same height in each room.|Note the wall material, distance from the router and any metal, water tank or microwave oven near the test spot because these reduce signal.|Test each spot three times and use the middle value so one odd reading does not mislead you.|Choose the router position that gives the weakest room an acceptable result, usually central and raised off the floor, and write down why.|A common mistake is to test only beside the router, which hides the rooms where the user actually has trouble.','A living room beside the router reads 90 Mbps and a corner bedroom behind two walls reads 45 Mbps. The bedroom gets 45 divided by 90, which is 50%, so it gets half the speed. Moving the router 4 metres towards the middle lifts the bedroom to 70 Mbps.','Produce a heat table listing every room, its three test readings, the middle value, the wall type and your chosen router position with one sentence of reasoning.','Your table covers every room with three readings each, shows the middle value correctly, and your router choice is backed by at least one measured improvement.','Why do you test each room three times and use the middle value?','One reading can be thrown off by a passing device or a brief interruption. Three readings with the middle value give a result you can trust and show the real condition of the room.'),
  M('Set up safely','Apply',20,'A new network is only safe if it is secured from the first minute. Default passwords and open settings are the most common way networks get misused. Careful setup and clear labels also make later support much easier.','Change the default router password and the default network name on day one, and store the new password somewhere the owner can find it.|Turn on the strongest encryption the router offers and choose a passphrase of at least 14 characters that is not a name or a date.|Create a separate guest network for visitors and smart devices so they cannot reach the laptops and phones on the main network.|Label both ends of every cable and record the router model, firmware version and settings in a one-page configuration sheet.|A common mistake is to leave remote management switched on, which lets outsiders try to reach the router settings.','A household has 12 devices. Moving 3 visitor devices and 2 smart plugs to the guest network leaves 7 devices on the main network, so 5 of 12 devices, about 42%, are kept apart from the private files.','Set up a small network with a secured main network and a guest network, then write a configuration sheet and tick each item of a safety checklist that you create.','Your sheet lists every setting changed, all default passwords are replaced, a guest network works and a mentor can reproduce the setup from your sheet alone.','Why create a separate guest network?','It keeps visitor and low-trust devices apart from the main devices, so a problem on a visitor phone cannot reach private laptops or files.'),
  M('Test and measure','Analyse',16,'Numbers beat feelings when you judge a service. You will take the same three tests at several times of day and compare them with what the provider promised. A pattern over time shows problems that one test would miss.','Run a speed test, a delay test and a packet-loss test, which are the three core measures of a connection.|Take each test at three times, for example morning, afternoon and evening, because shared links slow down at busy times.|Log each result in a table with the date, time, device and whether it was wired or wireless.|Compare the averages with the promised speed and state the gap as a percentage.|A common mistake is to run one test on a busy evening and call the whole service bad.','The plan promises 100 Mbps. The morning test gives 96 Mbps and the evening test gives 58 Mbps, so the average is (96 + 58) divided by 2, which is 77 Mbps. The gap against the promise is 100 minus 77, which is 23, divided by 100, or 23%. The evening reading is also 38 below the morning one, about 40% lower, so the service slows at busy time.','Run all three tests at three times of day over three days and complete the results table, then write two sentences describing the pattern.','Your table has at least 27 readings, the average and the percentage gap against the promise are calculated correctly, and your pattern statement matches the numbers.','Name the three tests you run and say what each one tells you.','Speed shows how much data arrives each second, delay shows how long a message takes to travel, and packet loss shows how much data goes missing on the way.'),
  M('Fault finding','Apply',20,'Most faults have simple causes, so you check the simple things first and only then dig deeper. Working in a fixed order stops you from changing many things at once and not knowing what fixed it. Every fault should end with a written record.','Start with the simplest checks in order: power light, cable seated at both ends, restart of the router and then the device.|Isolate the fault by swapping one part at a time, such as a different cable or a different device, and test after each swap.|Check the configuration last, because a setting that was changed earlier is a likely cause when nothing physical is wrong.|Write each fault in the log with the symptom, the cause found, the fix and the time it took.|A common mistake is to reset the router to factory settings straight away, which erases the secure setup you made.','A user reports drops every few minutes. Out of 200 test packets, 8 are lost, which is 8 divided by 200, or 4% loss. Reseating a loose network cable brings loss to 0 of 200, which proves the cable was the cause.','Find and fix three faults, which may be real or planted by your mentor, and log each with symptom, cause, fix and time taken.','All three faults are logged with a measured before and after result, and the fix for each was found by a one-change-at-a-time method.','What do you check first when a network fault is reported, and why?','Check power, cables and a restart first because simple causes are the most common and are quick to rule out before deeper work.'),
  M('Support users','Create',16,'Good support is clear writing and calm speaking. Users are not technical, so jargon makes them feel lost. A short guide and a simple call script help every user fix small problems without waiting for you.','Write a one-page guide in plain words with numbered steps and a picture of the router lights, so a user can follow it without help.|Prepare a short call script that follows four steps: ask what happened, check the basics, fix or guide the fix, and confirm it works.|Define escalation steps that say when a problem goes to a more skilled person and what information to pass on.|Avoid jargon and, where a technical word is needed, explain it in one short phrase the first time.|A common mistake is to say it should be fine now without asking the user to confirm the fault has actually gone.','Over a trial week you handle 12 support calls and resolve 9 on the first contact. That is 9 divided by 12, which is 75% first-contact resolution, and the 3 escalated calls are all written up with the details the next person needs.','Write the one-page user guide and the call script, then test them by asking a person outside the field to follow the guide and fix a planted fault.','The test user fixes the planted fault using only your guide in under 10 minutes, and your guide has no unexplained technical words.','Why must a support guide use plain words?','Users are usually not technical, so plain words let them act correctly and stop them from giving up or making the fault worse.'),
  M('30-day service','Apply',40,'You now run the service for real for 30 days. The aim is to keep it working and to record everything honestly, including outages. Honest records are what turn the work into evidence.','Do a short daily check of the router lights and one speed test, and write the result in the log.|Open a ticket for every complaint or outage and record the start time, end time, cause and fix.|Write a short weekly report with uptime, average speed, number of tickets and the main lesson.|Calculate uptime as the minutes the service was available divided by the total minutes in the period.|A common mistake is to leave out short outages, which makes the uptime figure look better than it really was.','A 30-day period holds 30 times 24 times 60, which is 43,200 minutes. One outage of 6 hours is 360 minutes. Uptime is 43,200 minus 360, which is 42,840, divided by 43,200, about 99.2%.','Run the service for 30 days and submit the daily log, the ticket log and four weekly reports, plus a final uptime calculation.','All 30 days are logged, every outage is in the ticket log and the uptime percentage is correct to one decimal place.','What is the uptime for one outage of 6 hours in a 30-day month?','The month has 43,200 minutes and the outage is 360 minutes, so uptime is 42,840 divided by 43,200, which is about 99.2%.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',4,'A review compares what you achieved with what you aimed for and decides what to change. It is short, honest and ends with dated actions. A plan with no dates is only a wish.','List your targets from the start of the project, such as uptime, average speed and time to fix, next to what you actually achieved.|Work out the gap for each target as a number or a percentage.|Write three lessons, each linked to a piece of evidence from your logs.|Choose the improvements that give the most benefit for the least effort and set a date for each.|A common mistake is to list improvements without saying how you will know they worked.','The target was 99.5% uptime and you reached 99.2%, a gap of 0.3 points. A 99.5% target allows 0.5% of 43,200 minutes, which is 216 minutes of outage, and you used 360, so you were 144 minutes over. A backup link is chosen as the main fix.','Present a five-minute review to your mentor and peer circle with a targets-against-results table, three lessons and a dated improvement plan.','Your table shows each gap correctly, each lesson cites a log entry and each improvement has an owner, a date and a way to measure success.','What should you do right after finishing a service report?','Choose the most valuable improvements, give each a date and decide how you will measure whether it worked.')])
S('SG-02','Freight and Courier Services','Plan, price and run a small courier or freight service with tracking, proof of delivery and honest costs. You learn the parcel journey, route planning, safe packing, restricted goods, cost per drop, proof of delivery and how to review failed deliveries.',
 'Plan and run a 30-day trial courier route for one partner client, record every delivery with proof, and report cost per drop, first-time delivery rate and claims handled.','About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. A defined route area, free map and spreadsheet tools, a partner client who agrees to the trial, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Route plan with times; price list with cost sheet; delivery log with proof for every drop; cost per drop; claims record; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain pickup, transit and delivery steps.','Plan a route and run drops.','Analyse cost, time and failures.','Create a price list and promise.'),
 ['Courier','Dispatcher assistant','Freight coordinator assistant','Warehouse assistant'],
 [M('The parcel journey','Understand',8,'Every parcel passes through the same chain of steps from the sender to the receiver. Each handover between steps is a place where a parcel can be delayed, damaged or lost. Seeing the whole chain helps you decide where to put checks.','The steps are pickup, sorting, transit, delivery and proof, and you should be able to say who holds the parcel at each step.|A handover is any moment the parcel changes hands or place, and handovers are where most losses happen.|A tracking update should be recorded at every step so you can say where a parcel was last seen.|A parcel needs a clear label with name, address, phone number and a reference number before it leaves the sender.|A common mistake is to scan a parcel only at pickup and delivery, which leaves the middle of the journey invisible.','Out of 200 parcels in a week, 3 go missing at the sorting step. That is 3 divided by 200, or 1.5%, and because all 3 were last scanned before sorting, the sorting step is where a new check is needed.','Draw the parcel journey for your own route as a flow chart, mark every handover and add one check you would put at each handover.','Your chart shows all five steps in order, marks every handover and gives a specific, practical check at each.','Where do parcels most often get lost, and why?','At handovers, because responsibility changes and a missed scan or label error means nobody knows where the parcel is.'),
  M('Route planning','Apply',16,'A good route uses fewer kilometres and causes fewer failed drops. You group stops by area, respect delivery time windows and leave a buffer for the unexpected. A route that looks perfect on paper but has no buffer will fail on the first busy day.','Group the drops by area on a map so the vehicle does not cross the same road several times.|Order the stops by their time windows first and by distance second.|Estimate stop time and drive time for each drop, using a test run for real numbers instead of guesses.|Add a buffer of about 20% of the total time for traffic, parking and failed attempts.|A common mistake is to plan for the fastest possible day and then promise times that only work with no delays.','You plan 10 drops. Each stop takes 8 minutes and each drive between stops takes 10 minutes, so each drop costs 18 minutes and 10 drops cost 180 minutes. A 20% buffer adds 36 minutes, so the planned route takes 216 minutes, or 3.6 hours.','Plan a route of at least 10 real drops with stop times, drive times, a buffer and a printed schedule, then drive it once to test it.','Your schedule shows times for every stop, a buffer of about 20%, and the test run finishes within 10% of the planned time.','Why do you add a buffer to a route?','Traffic, parking and failed attempts are normal, so a buffer keeps your promised times honest.'),
  M('Packing and handling','Apply',12,'The right packing protects the goods and keeps costs fair. Heavy or fragile items need specific care. Good labelling stops wrong deliveries.','Choose a box just big enough for the item so it cannot move and fill the gaps with paper or other filling.|Place heavy items at the bottom and light items on top, and mark fragile parcels clearly on two sides.|Calculate the size weight of a parcel as length times width times height in cm divided by the divisor your carrier uses, often 5,000, to get kilograms, and charge on whichever of actual weight or size weight is higher. Confirm the divisor with your carrier.|Put the address label flat on the largest side, with a second copy inside the parcel in case the outer label tears.|A common mistake is to use an oversized box, which raises the size weight and the cost.','A box measures 30 cm by 20 cm by 15 cm, which is 9,000 cubic cm. Divided by 5,000, the size weight is 1.8 kg. The actual weight is 1.2 kg, so the billed weight is 1.8 kg because it is the higher figure.','Pack three different test parcels, one heavy, one fragile and one oddly shaped, and complete a table of actual weight, size weight and billed weight for each.','All three parcels survive a one-metre carry and tilt test without damage, and your weight calculations are correct.','Why is a second label placed inside the parcel?','The outer label can tear or fall off, and an inside label still lets someone identify and deliver the parcel.'),
  M('Rules and dangerous goods','Apply',14,'Some goods are banned or restricted, such as certain batteries, liquids and sharp items. You must not carry anything you cannot identify. Rules differ by place, so you always confirm them.','Keep a written list of banned and restricted goods taken from the official source and review it before every new client.|Ask the sender what is inside every parcel and write the answer on the booking record.|Do not carry any item you cannot identify or that the sender will not describe, and hold it until it is checked.|Keep records of what you refused and why, with the date and sender, so you can show good practice.|Check with your local authority for the current rules before you accept any new type of goods.','In a batch of 40 parcels, 3 are flagged by the list as restricted. They are held for checking, which is 3 divided by 40, or 7.5% of the batch, and the other 37 go out on time.','Write a one-page checklist for accepting parcels, including questions to ask senders and the steps to take when an item is unclear, and confirm it with your local authority.','Your checklist has at least eight steps, includes a hold-and-check step for unknown items and shows evidence that you asked your local authority.','What should you do with a parcel when you are unsure of the contents?','Do not carry it, hold it, and get the contents and rules checked first.'),
  M('Price and cost','Evaluate',16,'Your price must cover what each drop really costs. Cost per drop is the number that decides whether you make or lose money. Knowing it lets you set a price and a margin with confidence.','List the costs of one day: fuel, vehicle wear, insurance share, your time and packing materials.|Calculate cost per drop as the total cost for the period divided by the number of drops in the period.|Add a margin on top of cost so the price covers surprises, and state the margin as a percentage of the price.|Use all your real figures from the trial route instead of rough guesses, and update them each week.|A common mistake is to leave out your own time and vehicle wear, which makes the service look cheaper than it is.','A day costs S$76 in total for 20 drops, so cost per drop is 76 divided by 20, which is S$3.80. A price of S$5 leaves S$1.20 per drop, and 1.20 divided by 5 is a 24% margin.','Complete a cost sheet for your route showing every cost, the cost per drop, a proposed price and the margin percentage.','Your cost per drop and margin are calculated correctly from recorded figures, and a mentor can follow each number back to a receipt or note.','What is the cost per drop when a day costs S$76 and you make 20 drops?','76 divided by 20 gives S$3.80 per drop.'),
  M('Proof and tracking','Create',12,'Proof of delivery ends arguments about whether a parcel arrived. It should be quick for you and clear for the customer. Tracking updates keep customers informed and reduce calls.','Choose a proof method such as a photo at the door or a signature, and agree it with the client before you start.|Record the time and place with every proof so a dispute can be checked.|Send a short status message to the receiver at pickup and again when you are about 15 minutes away.|Store proofs safely for an agreed period and delete them afterwards, because they can show private addresses, and check the current data-protection rules with your local authority.|A common mistake is to take a photo that does not show the parcel and the door number together.','On a test day of 50 drops, 46 have a clear photo with a time stamp. That is 46 divided by 50, or 92%, and the 4 without proof are all noted as handed to a neighbour with a name.','Design a proof process on one page with the method, the data you record, how you store it and how you handle a missing proof, then test it on 10 drops.','Your process is tested on at least 10 drops, at least 90% of them have clear proof, and the privacy handling is written down.','Why do you take proof of delivery?','It settles disputes by showing who received the parcel, where and when.'),
  M('30-day trial','Apply',40,'You now run the route for 30 days and keep a complete record. The record shows what works and what does not. Honest numbers matter more than perfect ones.','Log each drop with the time, the result, the proof and the cost, and keep the log up to date every day.|Record every failed attempt with the cause, such as nobody home or wrong address.|Record every claim for damage or loss with the date, the amount and how it was settled.|Calculate first-time delivery rate as successful first attempts divided by total drops.|A common mistake is to remove failed deliveries from the log, which hides the real success rate.','Of 50 drops in one week, 47 are delivered on the first attempt. First-time delivery rate is 47 divided by 50, which is 0.94, or 94%, and the 3 failures are logged with causes.','Run your route for 30 days and submit the full delivery log, the failure list, the claims record and a weekly first-time delivery rate.','The log covers all 30 days, every failed attempt has a cause and your first-time delivery rates are calculated correctly.','What is the first-time delivery rate if 47 of 50 parcels are delivered at the first attempt?','47 divided by 50 is 0.94, so the rate is 94%.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',12,'The review turns 30 days of records into a short list of fixes. You look at what failed, why it failed and what would stop it happening again. Small fixes that tackle the biggest cause give the best return.','Group failed deliveries by cause and count each group.|Work out which cause accounts for the largest share and focus your first fix there.|Estimate the saving from each fix in drops or money so you can compare them.|Choose three fixes, give each an owner and a date, and decide how you will measure success.|A common mistake is to fix the loudest complaint instead of the most frequent cause.','Over the trial, 3 of 50 drops fail, which is 6%. Two were nobody home and one was a wrong address. An SMS alert that halves the nobody-home failures saves 1 drop, taking failures to 2 of 50, or 4%.','Present a ten-minute review with a causes table, three fixes with dates and a revised price list if costs have changed.','Your causes table adds up to the total failures and each fix names the cause it targets, a date and a measure.','What reduces failed attempts most cheaply?','Alerts to the receiver and agreed time windows, because they target the most common cause of missed deliveries.')])
S('SG-03','Education and Learning Services','Design and teach a short learning programme to a small group and measure what they learned. You learn to write checkable outcomes, find out what learners already know, plan active sessions, teach clearly, assess fairly and compare results before and after.',
 'Design, teach and assess a 4-session programme for a small group and report learning gain with a plan for the next version.','About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. A small group of consenting learners, free tools, a mentor teacher and a peer circle.',
 'Outcomes written; four session plans; assessment with rubric; learner feedback; learning-gain report with calculations; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain how outcomes, activity and check fit.','Teach a short programme.','Analyse learning gain.','Create session plans and checks.'),
 ['Learning assistant','Tutor','Training coordinator','Content assistant'],
 [M('Outcomes','Understand',10,'An outcome says what learners will be able to do at the end, and it is the starting point for everything else. If you cannot check an outcome, you cannot tell whether teaching worked. Good outcomes begin with an action verb.','Begin each outcome with an action verb such as calculate, list, compare or build, because these describe visible behaviour.|Write one outcome for each session so each session has a single clear aim.|Say how you will check each outcome, for example a short task or a question with a standard to reach.|Avoid vague words such as understand or know when you write the outcome, because you cannot see them.|A common mistake is to write what you will teach instead of what the learner will do.','The outcome reads: calculate a 20% discount. Checking it, a learner finds that 20% of S$45 is S$9, so the new price is S$36, and the answer can be marked right or wrong.','Write four outcomes, one for each session of your programme, and next to each write the exact task that will check it.','Each outcome starts with an action verb, each is paired with a checkable task, and a peer confirms they can tell when it has been met.','Why should outcomes start with an action verb?','Action verbs describe something visible, so you can tell whether the outcome was reached.'),
  M('Know your learners','Analyse',10,'Learners arrive with different knowledge, languages and needs. A short pre-check shows where to start and what to skip. Without it, you may bore some learners and lose others.','Give a short pre-check of about five questions on the topic before teaching begins.|Mark the pre-check and record each learner score and the questions most often missed.|Note the language, reading level and any support needs that learners share with you.|Adjust your plan by spending more time on the weakest areas and less on what most already know.|A common mistake is to use the pre-check score to label a learner instead of to plan the teaching.','Eight learners take a five-question pre-check on fractions, and only 4 get question 3 correct. That is 4 divided by 8, or 50%, so fractions get extra time in session one.','Run a pre-check with your learners, record each result in a table and write a short paragraph on how it changes your session plans.','Your table shows results for every learner, you identify the most-missed question and you list at least two changes to your plans.','Why give a pre-check before teaching?','It shows what learners already know so you start at the right level.'),
  M('Plan a session','Create',16,'A session plan lays out what happens, when and why. Short, active sessions work better than long talks. A timed plan keeps you honest about what fits.','Use four parts in each session: a hook to get attention, a short teach, practice and a check.|Keep talking to about 10 minutes at a time and use the rest for learners to practise.|Give a time to each part and make sure the times add up to the length of the session.|List the materials and prepare them in advance so no time is lost.|A common mistake is to plan too much content, which leaves no time for practice or a check.','A 50-minute session is planned as a 5-minute hook, 10 minutes of teaching, 25 minutes of practice and a 10-minute check. The parts add to 5 plus 10 plus 25 plus 10, which is 50 minutes, and half of the time is practice.','Write four session plans, each with a time for every part, the materials and the check task that matches the outcome.','Every plan adds up to its stated length, has at least half the time for practice and each check task matches its outcome.','Why should most of a session be practice?','People learn by doing, so practice is where the learning is made stronger.'),
  M('Teaching skills','Apply',16,'Teaching clearly takes plain words, checking and patience. You speak simply, ask questions and wait for answers. Practising with a peer helps you improve before you teach the group.','Use short, plain sentences and explain any new word the first time you use it.|Check understanding by asking learners to explain the idea back in their own words.|Wait at least three seconds after asking a question before you speak, so learners have time to think.|Praise effort and give one specific tip for improvement to each learner.|A common mistake is to ask do you understand and accept silence as yes.','You explain a method and ask two of the 8 learners to explain it back. One gets a step wrong, which shows that 1 of 2 checked learners needs it re-taught before practice starts.','Teach one full session to a peer or the group while a mentor observes, and complete a short self-review of what went well and what to change.','The observer confirms you used plain words, checked understanding at least twice and gave learners time to answer.','How can you check that learners understand?','Ask them to explain it back or show it, instead of asking if they understand.'),
  M('Assessment','Create',16,'An assessment shows whether the outcome was met. A fair assessment has a clear task, a condition and a standard. A rubric makes the marking fair and the feedback useful.','State the task, the conditions such as time and tools, and the standard such as 70% correct.|Write a rubric with clear descriptions for each mark band so two markers would give the same result.|Make the assessment match the outcome, so if the outcome says calculate, the task asks for a calculation.|Give written feedback within 48 hours so learners can use it.|A common mistake is to assess something you did not teach.','An assessment has 20 marks and a pass standard of 70%. The pass mark is 70% of 20, which is 14 marks, so a learner who scores 13 is one mark short and receives feedback on exactly what to improve.','Build an assessment with a task, a rubric, a marking sheet and a feedback template, then have a peer mark one sample answer to test it.','Your peer and you give marks within 2 marks of each other on the same sample and the rubric covers every mark band.','Why use a rubric when marking?','It makes marking fair and clear, and it lets learners see what to improve.'),
  M('Teach the programme','Apply',30,'Now you teach all four sessions and keep notes. You adjust as you go, based on what you see. Learner voice helps you improve each session.','Take attendance at every session and write short notes on what worked and what did not straight after teaching.|Adjust the next session when you see that learners struggled, for example by adding an example or more practice time.|Ask learners for one thing that helped and one thing to change at the end of each session.|Keep learner data private and store names and marks safely, following the consent you were given.|A common mistake is to keep to the plan when the group is clearly lost.','Over four sessions with 8 learners there are 32 possible attendances. Attendance is 29, so it is 29 divided by 32, which is about 90.6%, and the three absences are followed up with a catch-up task.','Teach all four sessions and submit the attendance table, your session notes and learner comments, with at least one documented adjustment.','Attendance is recorded for every session, at least one adjustment is linked to a specific observation and learner data is stored securely.','Why adjust a plan while teaching?','Because learners differ, and changing the plan to fit them improves their learning.'),
  M('Learning gain','Analyse',12,'Learning gain compares the results before and after teaching. It shows whether the programme made a difference. You also look at who did not gain, because they show what to fix.','Use the same or a very similar test before and after, so the comparison is fair.|Calculate the average score before and the average score after for the group.|Calculate gain as the after average minus the before average, and also look at each learner gain.|Look closely at learners with little or no gain and look for reasons such as absence or unclear teaching.|A common mistake is to report only the average, which hides learners who did not improve.','The group average rises from 45 to 72 out of 100, so the gain is 72 minus 45, which is 27 points. The largest possible gain was 100 minus 45, which is 55, so the group achieved 27 divided by 55, or about 49%, of what was possible.','Calculate the before and after averages, the gain for the group and for each learner, and write a short report with one chart.','Your calculations are correct, a table shows each learner, and you name at least one possible reason for any learner with a gain below 10 points.','What is the gain when the average goes from 45 to 72?','72 minus 45 is a gain of 27 points.'),
  M('Improve','Evaluate',10,'Improving means using your evidence to make the next version better. You pick a few changes, test them and share what you learned. Learners and peers can help.','Study your gain table and your feedback to find the biggest weakness.|Choose up to three changes, such as a practice quiz, an extra example or a different order of topics.|For each change say what result you expect and how you will measure it.|Share your sample session plan and results with peers so they can learn from your work.|A common mistake is to change everything at once, because then you cannot tell what helped.','Question 4 on the after test was missed by 6 of 8 learners, which is 6 divided by 8, or 75%. You choose to add a worked example and a practice quiz on that topic and aim for 75% of learners to get it right next time.','Present a plan for version two with three changes, expected results, measures and dates.','Each change is linked to evidence from your results and has a measure and a date.','What should drive the changes you make to a programme?','The results and feedback from learners, not your guesses.')],
 safety="Learning only. Get consent from every learner or guardian, protect learner data, follow child-protection rules if you teach minors, and check with your local authority for the rules that apply to you.")
S('SG-04','Travel and Tourism Services','Design a small local tour or travel service, price it, run it with a test group and learn from feedback. You learn about guest needs, itinerary design, safety planning, pricing, accurate storytelling, running a tour and using feedback.',
 'Design and run one small guided experience for a test group and report safety, cost and guest satisfaction with a plan for the next version.','About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. A local route, free tools, a test group, a licensed partner where needed, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Itinerary with timings; risk plan; price sheet with break-even; run record; guest feedback summary; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain what guests value on a trip.','Run a small tour.','Analyse cost and satisfaction.','Create an itinerary and risk plan.'),
 ['Tour assistant','Guest service officer','Travel planner','Local guide helper'],
 [M('Know the guest','Understand',8,'Different guests want different things from the same place. Family groups, solo travellers, seniors and first-time visitors all need different pace, comfort and information. Knowing the guest shapes every later decision.','Describe at least three guest types, such as a family with children, a senior group and a solo visitor, and list what each values most.|Note the time and budget each type usually has, because these limit what you can offer.|List access needs such as step-free routes, seating, shade and toilets for each type.|Ask real people from each type two or three questions, and use their answers instead of assumptions.|A common mistake is to design the tour for yourself instead of for the guest.','A senior group of 12 needs a seat about every 20 minutes. A 2-hour walk is 120 minutes, so you need 120 divided by 20, which is 6 seating points, and you add them to the route.','Write three guest profiles of about 80 words each with needs, time, budget and access points, based on at least one short conversation each.','Your profiles each list at least three specific needs and lead to at least one concrete change in the itinerary.','Why write guest profiles?','They show what each type of guest needs, so the plan can be shaped around them.'),
  M('Design the experience','Create',16,'A good experience has a story, a route and a comfortable pace. You choose a theme and place the stops so the time works. A rain plan makes the tour dependable.','Pick a clear theme, such as local food or old buildings, and choose four or five stops that fit the theme.|Time each stop and each walk and add a buffer of about 10 to 15 minutes for delays.|Plan the pace around the slowest guest, not the fastest.|Write a rain plan with a covered alternative for at least two stops.|A common mistake is to pack in too many stops, leaving guests tired and rushed.','A heritage walk of 120 minutes has 5 stops of 10 minutes each, which is 50 minutes, plus 55 minutes of walking and a 15-minute buffer. The parts add to 50 plus 55 plus 15, which is 120 minutes.','Draft a full itinerary with times, a route map, a rain plan and notes on access for your guest profiles.','Your itinerary adds up to the stated time with a buffer, and a peer can follow it from your map and notes.','Why include a rain plan?','Weather is unpredictable, and a plan keeps the tour safe and enjoyable when it changes.'),
  M('Safety and rules','Apply',16,'Safety comes before fun. You list the risks, plan how to reduce them and know who to call. Some activities need permits or licensed partners, so you check the rules first.','List the main risks for your route, such as heat, traffic, uneven ground and crowding, and rate each as low, medium or high.|For each risk write a control, such as a water break every hour or a guide at the back of the group.|Carry a first-aid kit, a charged phone and a card with emergency numbers and the nearest medical help.|Keep to a guest-to-guide ratio of about 10 to 1 unless your local authority or licensed partner sets a different one, so 24 guests need 3 guides because 24 divided by 10 is 2.4 and you round up.|Check with your local authority about permits and licensed partners before you run any paid or public activity.','A test group of 24 guests needs 24 divided by 10, which is 2.4, rounded up to 3 guides. With only 2 available you reduce the group to 20 guests for the trial.','Write a risk plan with at least eight risks, controls, an emergency contact card and a note of permits you confirmed with your local authority.','Every risk has a control and an owner, the contact card is complete and a mentor has reviewed the plan before the test tour.','What is the first step in planning safety?','Make a list of the risks and the emergency contacts.'),
  M('Price','Evaluate',14,'A price should cover your costs and still feel fair to guests. You separate costs that stay the same from costs that change with each guest. Break-even tells you how many guests you need.','List fixed costs, such as guide time and printing, and costs per guest, such as a drink or an entry ticket.|Work out the contribution per guest, which is the price minus the cost per guest.|Calculate break-even guests as fixed costs divided by contribution per guest, and round up.|Add a margin and compare your price with similar local offers.|A common mistake is to forget small costs, such as insurance share and printing, which add up.','Fixed costs are S$120 and the cost per guest is S$4. At a price of S$15, each guest adds S$11. Break-even is 120 divided by 11, which is 10.9, so you need 11 guests.','Complete a price sheet with fixed costs, per-guest costs, break-even guests and a recommended price, and show the working.','Your break-even number is correct, every cost is recorded and the recommended price clearly covers costs at the expected group size.','What is the break-even for fixed costs of S$120 and S$11 contribution per guest?','120 divided by 11 is about 10.9, so you need 11 guests.'),
  M('Tell the story','Create',12,'Guests trust a guide who is accurate and respectful. Your script uses true facts, respects local culture and is easy to follow. Short stories work better than long lists.','Check each fact with at least two reliable sources and note where it came from.|Respect local customs by asking permission for photos and by describing communities in their own terms.|Keep each stop to a story of about two minutes with one clear message.|Use plain, accessible language and offer a written summary for guests who prefer to read.|A common mistake is to repeat a popular claim without checking it.','Your script has 8 facts. You can confirm 7 with two sources each, which is 7 divided by 8, or 87.5%. The eighth is removed until you can confirm it.','Write a script for each stop with its sources, then rehearse it once with a peer who gives feedback.','All facts in the final script have two sources noted, and the peer confirms the script was clear and respectful.','Why check every fact in a guide script?','Guests trust accurate guides, and false claims harm your reputation and the places you describe.'),
  M('Run the test tour','Apply',30,'The test tour is a real run with a small group. You record numbers, times and anything that goes wrong. Honest records show what to improve.','Count guests at the start and at each stop so no one is lost.|Record the actual start and finish times and compare them with the plan.|Write down every incident, such as a missed step or a safety concern, with what you did.|Brief guests at the start on safety rules, the route and the emergency plan.|A common mistake is to keep going when the group is struggling instead of changing the route.','The tour was planned for 120 minutes and took 130 minutes, so it ran 10 minutes late. That is 10 divided by 120, or about 8.3%, and the cause was a longer stop at stop 3.','Run the test tour with at least 6 guests and submit a record of headcounts, times and incidents with your actions.','Your record has times for every stop, counts the group at each stop and shows an action for each incident.','What should you record on a test tour?','Guest counts, times and any incidents.'),
  M('Feedback','Analyse',16,'Feedback tells you how guests felt. Short, specific questions give better answers than a vague one. You compare the results with your goals.','Ask guests to score value, safety and the guide from 1 to 5, and give space for one comment.|Collect the forms straight after the tour while memories are fresh.|Calculate the average for each score and note the lowest one.|Read the comments and group them into themes, such as pace, information or comfort.|A common mistake is to read only the praise and skip the comments about problems.','Ten guests give value scores of 4, 5, 4, 4, 3, 5, 4, 4, 5 and 4. The total is 42, so the average is 42 divided by 10, which is 4.2.','Collect feedback from at least 80% of your guests, calculate the average score for each question and write a summary of three themes.','Your averages are correct, you reached at least 80% of guests and your themes are backed by quotes or counts.','Which three scores should a tour feedback form include?','Value, safety and the guide.'),
  M('Improve','Evaluate',18,'Improvement means choosing the fixes that matter most and testing them. You use costs and guest scores to decide. Small changes, tested one by one, work best.','Choose the issue with the lowest score or most comments as the first fix.|Check the cost of each fix and compare it with the expected benefit.|Test a revised price to see whether guests still rate the value highly.|Plan how to reach more guests in a way that is honest and safe.|A common mistake is to raise the price without checking guest value scores first.','With 15 guests at S$15 you earn S$225 and costs are S$120 plus 15 times S$4, which is S$180, so profit is S$45. A shade stop adds S$20 cost, which cuts profit to S$25 but raises comfort scores.','Present a plan for the next run with a fix, a cost, a price decision and a date.','Your plan links each decision to data from your cost sheet or feedback and includes a measure for the next run.','What should you use to decide whether to change the price?','Your costs and the guest value scores.')])
S('SG-05','Transport and Mobility Services','Plan a small transport or mobility service, check safety and cost, and trial it with real passengers or goods. You learn to read demand, build a timetable, check licences and vehicles, work out cost per trip, protect privacy and analyse delays.',
 'Plan and trial one small mobility service for 30 days and report safety checks, punctuality, passenger counts and cost per trip, with a ranked improvement plan.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. A licensed partner vehicle or public transit data, free tools, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Route and timetable design; safety and rule check; punctuality data; cost per trip; improvement plan; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain demand, supply and timetable.','Plan and record trips.','Analyse punctuality and cost.','Create an improvement plan.'),
 ['Transport coordinator assistant','Dispatcher assistant','Mobility planner assistant','Operations analyst assistant'],
 [M('Demand and supply','Understand',10,'Demand is how many people want to travel and supply is how many seats you offer. They rarely match all day. Counting trips by hour shows where the gaps are.','Count the number of people travelling in each hour of the day on at least two ordinary days.|Compare the count with the seats available in that hour to find the load percentage.|Mark the hours where demand is above 100% of seats and the hours where seats are mostly empty.|Look for reasons behind peaks, such as school start times or work shifts.|A common mistake is to plan from the daily total, which hides the busy hours.','From 8 to 9 am, 62 people want a ride and the bus has 50 seats. Load is 62 divided by 50, which is 1.24, or 124%, so 12 people are left behind and the peak needs extra capacity.','Count trips for a full day on each of two ordinary days at one stop or on one route, and produce an hourly table with seats, demand and load percentage.','Your table covers every service hour, the load percentages are correct and you name the two busiest and two quietest hours.','Why do you count trips by the hour?','Demand changes through the day, and hourly counts show where seats are short or empty.'),
  M('Routes and timetable','Create',16,'A good route is simple and a good timetable is regular. Stops should be close to where people need to go. Even gaps between services are easy to remember.','Place stops within a short walk of homes, schools and shops, and avoid detours that add little demand.|Use an even gap, such as every 15 minutes, so passengers can remember the times without looking.|Add a layover at each end so late arrivals do not delay the next trip.|Work out the vehicles you need as round-trip time including layover divided by the gap, rounded up.|A common mistake is to schedule trips with no layover, so one delay passes down the whole day.','A 15-minute gap gives 4 departures an hour, or 12 departures in 3 hours. A round trip takes 40 minutes plus 5 minutes layover, which is 45 minutes, so you need 45 divided by 15, which is 3 vehicles.','Draft a timetable for one route with stop times, gaps, layover and the number of vehicles needed, and explain your choices in a short note.','Your timetable has even gaps, shows the layover and vehicle calculation correctly and a peer can read it without help.','Why use even gaps between services?','Passengers can remember the pattern and plan without looking up each time.'),
  M('Safety and licensing','Apply',16,'Only licensed drivers and vehicles in good condition may carry passengers. You check licences and run daily vehicle inspections. An emergency plan is ready before the first trip.','Check each driver licence and the vehicle documents against the rules, and note the expiry dates.|Run a daily inspection of at least 12 items, such as brakes, lights, tyres, seat belts and doors.|Do not run a vehicle that fails an item that affects safety, and record what was done.|Write an emergency plan with contact numbers, a first-aid kit location and steps for a breakdown or accident.|Check with your local authority about the licences, insurance and rules for carrying passengers or goods.','A daily inspection has 12 items and 11 pass. The brake light fails, so the vehicle does not run until it is repaired, and the record shows the fault, the fix and who signed it off.','Create a daily checklist, a licence record sheet and an emergency plan, then complete one practice inspection on a real vehicle with the owner.','Your checklist covers at least 12 items, the practice inspection is signed by the owner and your plan is confirmed with your local authority.','Who is responsible for checking licences and vehicles?','The operator must check them, and the local authority sets and enforces the rules.'),
  M('Cost per trip','Evaluate',16,'Knowing your cost per trip lets you set a fare and see if the service can last. Costs include fuel or energy, the driver, upkeep and insurance. You divide by trips and by passengers.','List the costs for a period: fuel or charging, driver time, repairs and tyres, insurance share and booking or admin.|Divide the total cost by the number of trips to get cost per trip.|Divide cost per trip by the passengers on average to get cost per passenger.|Compare cost per passenger with the fare and decide whether the service covers its costs.|A common mistake is to ignore upkeep and insurance because they are not paid every day.','One trip costs S$18 and carries 10 passengers, so cost per passenger is 18 divided by 10, which is S$1.80. A fare of S$3 brings in S$30 for the trip, which leaves S$12 above cost.','Complete a cost sheet for your trial with each cost, cost per trip, cost per passenger and break-even passengers per trip.','Your calculations are right, every cost has a source and you show the break-even number of passengers per trip.','What is the cost per passenger when a trip costs S$18 and carries 10 people?','18 divided by 10 is S$1.80 per passenger.'),
  M('Run the trial','Apply',36,'You now run the service and record every trip. Honest data on lateness, loads and incidents is the base for improvement. Do not tidy the numbers.','Record the planned and actual departure and arrival times for every trip.|Count passengers on each trip and note the stop where the loading was heaviest.|Record every incident or complaint with the time, place and what was done.|Calculate punctuality as the trips on time divided by the total trips, and decide in advance what on time means, such as within 3 minutes.|A common mistake is to change the definition of on time after seeing the results.','Of 100 trips in the trial, 82 arrive within 3 minutes of schedule. Punctuality is 82 divided by 100, which is 82%, and the 18 late trips are listed with the cause.','Run the trial for 30 days and submit a full trip log, a punctuality calculation and an incident list.','Every trip is logged with times and passenger counts, and your punctuality figure is calculated using your original definition.','What is punctuality if 82 of 100 trips are on time?','82 divided by 100 is 82%.'),
  M('Data and privacy','Apply',12,'Passenger data can reveal where people live and work. You collect only what you need and keep it safe. Counting is better than naming.','Collect counts of passengers, not names, unless a name is needed for the service.|Store the data in a protected file and limit who can open it.|Delete data you no longer need after the agreed period and note the date you did so.|Tell passengers what you count and why in a short, plain notice.|Check with your local authority about the data protection rules that apply to you.','Over a week you record 480 anonymous tap counts across 5 stops. There are no names, so the 480 counts show demand, but they cannot be linked to a person.','Write a one-page data note covering what you collect, why, how it is stored, who sees it and when it is deleted.','Your note covers all five points, collects no more than needed and has been reviewed by your mentor.','Why count passengers instead of recording who they are?','Counts give the information you need while protecting personal privacy.'),
  M('Analyse delays','Analyse',16,'To fix delays you first find where and why they happen. A table of delay by stop and by cause points to the real problem. Fix the cause, not the symptom.','Calculate the delay at each stop as actual time minus planned time and record it for every trip.|Add up the delays by stop and find the stop where most delay builds up.|List the causes you saw, such as slow loading, traffic or late start, and count each one.|Rank the causes by how many late trips each one explains.|A common mistake is to blame traffic for everything without checking the data.','Of 100 trips, 18 were late. At stop 4, 11 of those 18 lost time to slow loading, which is 11 divided by 18, or about 61%, so stop 4 is where to look first.','Build a delay table by stop and by cause, draw one chart and write two sentences naming the main cause and the evidence for it.','Your table totals match your trip log, the chart is labelled with units and your main cause is supported by at least 50% of late trips.','What is the first step in finding why trips are late?','Find where along the route the delay happens.'),
  M('Improve','Evaluate',18,'Improvement is about picking the fixes that give the most benefit for the cost. You compare options, choose, and set a date. You also decide how you will measure success.','List at least three possible fixes and the number of late trips each could remove.|Estimate the cost of each fix in money and effort.|Divide the benefit by the cost to rank the fixes, and choose the best one or two.|Write next steps with dates and the measure of success for each.|A common mistake is to pick the cheapest fix even when it solves little.','Fix A removes 6 late trips a week for S$40 and fix B removes 2 for S$5. Fix A gives 6 divided by 40, or 0.15 per S$1, and fix B gives 2 divided by 5, or 0.4 per S$1, so B is better value although A removes more.','Present an improvement plan with at least three fixes ranked by impact against cost and a timeline.','Your ranking uses numbers from your trial and each chosen fix has a date and a measure.','What ranks fixes best?','Their impact compared with their cost.')])
S('SG-06','E-commerce Operations','Run the back end of a small online shop: stock, orders, packing, returns and customer service. You learn the order-to-delivery cycle, stock control, accurate picking and packing, shipping cutoffs, returns, service replies and error analysis.',
 'Run order handling for a small online shop for 30 days and report accuracy, speed, returns and service replies, with a plan to cut the top error cause.','About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. A small shop partner, a free spreadsheet, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Stock system; order log; accuracy and speed numbers; returns handled; service replies; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain the order-to-delivery cycle.','Handle orders accurately.','Analyse errors and returns.','Design a stock and returns process.'),
 ['Order processor','Warehouse assistant','Customer service agent','E-commerce coordinator'],
 [M('Order cycle','Understand',8,'An online order goes through a cycle from the customer clicking buy to the product arriving and maybe coming back. Each step can fail. Knowing the cycle shows where to put checks.','The cycle has six steps: order, pick, pack, ship, deliver and return.|Each step has a person or system responsible and a typical time, which you should record.|Errors early in the cycle, such as picking the wrong item, cause costly returns later.|Time each step for a sample of orders to find the slowest step.|A common mistake is to look only at shipping speed and ignore errors in picking.','Out of 50 orders in a test week, 3 are returned because the wrong size was picked. That is 3 divided by 50, or 6%, and all three trace back to the pick step.','Map the cycle for your shop as a flow chart with the person, the tool and the typical time at each step.','Your map shows all six steps with owners and times, and you mark at least two places where errors are likely.','Name the steps in the order cycle.','Order, pick, pack, ship, deliver and return.'),
  M('Stock','Apply',14,'Good stock control means you know what you have and where it is. You count regularly and reorder in time. Running out loses sales and holding too much ties up money.','Give every item a bin or shelf label and a code that matches your sales list.|Count stock each week and fix any difference between the count and the record.|Calculate the reorder point as daily sales times the days to restock plus a safety amount.|Record each reorder with the date, quantity and supplier.|A common mistake is to trust the stock record without counting.','An item sells 4 units a day and takes 3 days to restock. Lead-time demand is 4 times 3, which is 12 units. Adding a safety amount of 8 gives a reorder point of 20 units.','Set up a stock sheet for at least 15 items with bin labels, counts and reorder points, then do a count and fix any differences.','Your count matches the record for at least 90% of items after correction, and each reorder point has a worked calculation.','What is a reorder point?','The stock level that triggers a new order so you do not run out.'),
  M('Pick and pack','Apply',16,'Picking is finding the right item and packing is protecting it for delivery. Small checks at this stage stop most errors. Speed comes after accuracy.','Use a pick list that shows the item code, location and quantity for each order line.|Check the item against the order by scanning or by reading the code aloud before it goes in the box.|Pack with filling so the item cannot move, and add a packing slip with the order number.|Measure accuracy as the number of correct orders divided by the total orders.|A common mistake is to speed up without checking, which raises errors and returns.','You pick and pack 20 test orders and 19 are correct. Accuracy is 19 divided by 20, which is 95%, and the one wrong order had a similar looking colour.','Pick and pack at least 20 real or practice orders, record the time and errors for each, and calculate accuracy and average time per order.','Your accuracy is at least 95%, you recorded the time of each order and you can say what caused each error.','What is the accuracy if 19 of 20 orders are correct?','19 divided by 20 is 95%.'),
  M('Ship and track','Apply',12,'Shipping is about keeping a promise on time. A daily cutoff and tracking numbers help customers know when to expect delivery. Delays should be told early.','Set a daily cutoff time, such as 2 pm, and tell customers what it means for their order.|Hand parcels to the carrier at the same time each day and keep the receipt.|Send tracking information to the customer within an hour of dispatch.|Contact customers early when a parcel is delayed and offer a clear next step.|A common mistake is to promise next-day delivery for orders placed after the cutoff.','On a test day, 40 orders come in and 34 arrive before the 2 pm cutoff. So 34 divided by 40, or 85%, ship the same day, and the other 6 are told they will ship the next day.','Ship at least 10 test orders and complete a log with cutoff time, dispatch time, tracking number and the message sent.','Your log shows every order dispatched on its promised day and every customer message sent within an hour.','Why use a daily cutoff?','It makes delivery promises reliable.'),
  M('Returns','Apply',14,'Returns are part of online selling. A clear policy and a quick, fair process keeps customers and saves money. You also learn from why items come back.','Write a returns policy in plain words with the time limit, condition required and how refunds are made.|Inspect each returned item and record the reason and the condition.|Refund or swap within a stated time, such as 5 days after the item arrives.|Put returned goods back into stock only if they are in saleable condition.|A common mistake is to keep no record of reasons, which loses useful information.','Out of 80 orders, 4 are returned, which is 4 divided by 80, or 5%. Each refund is S$24, so refunds total 4 times 24, which is S$96, and 3 of the 4 are for size problems.','Handle at least five test returns and keep a log with the reason, condition, decision, refund amount and time taken.','Every return has a recorded reason, all are processed within your stated time and you identify the most common reason.','Why have a clear returns policy?','It reduces disputes because everyone knows the rules.'),
  M('Customer service','Create',16,'Good service means a fast, kind and helpful reply. Template answers save time but must still feel personal. You also know when to pass a case to someone else.','Aim to reply to every message within 24 hours and track the time to first reply.|Write template answers for common questions such as delay, wrong item and refund status.|Add the customer name and order number to every reply so it feels personal.|Pass a case to a senior person when it involves a safety issue, a legal threat or an amount above your limit.|A common mistake is to copy a template without checking it fits the question.','You receive 25 messages in a week and reply to 23 within 24 hours. That is 23 divided by 25, or 92%, and the 2 late replies were sent over a weekend.','Write at least six template replies and answer 10 test messages, recording the reply time for each.','Your templates are clear and polite, at least 90% of replies are within 24 hours and each reply includes the order number.','What is a good target for the first reply?','Within 24 hours.'),
  M('30 days','Apply',40,'You now run the shop operations for 30 days. Keep a complete log of orders, errors and costs. Honest numbers are the base for improvement.','Record every order with the pick time, pack time, ship time and any error.|Record every return and every complaint with a reason.|Add up the cost of packing materials, labour time and shipping for each order.|Calculate the error rate as orders with errors divided by total orders.|A common mistake is to stop recording when the work gets busy.','In the first week there are 95 orders and 2 have errors. The error rate is 2 divided by 95, which is about 2.1%, and both errors were a wrong size.','Run order handling for 30 days and submit the order log, the error and returns log and a weekly summary with the error rate.','Your log covers all 30 days, the weekly error rates are correct and each error has a cause.','What is the error rate if 2 of 95 orders have errors?','2 divided by 95 is about 2.1%.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',10,'The review looks at your data and decides what to fix first. You find the biggest cause of errors and plan a simple fix. Then you measure again.','Group errors by cause, such as wrong size, wrong item or damage, and count each group.|Find the cause that creates the most errors and focus on it first.|Choose a simple, low-cost fix, such as a scan check or a better label.|Set a target, such as halving that error cause, and a date to re-measure.|A common mistake is to fix several things at once and not know which worked.','Of 12 errors in a month, 7 were wrong size, which is 7 divided by 12, or about 58%. A scan check at packing could remove most of them, so you set a goal of fewer than 4 wrong size errors next month, which is about half of 7.','Present a plan showing your error causes, the chosen fix, the target and the date for the next review.','Your plan targets the largest cause with a fix, a numeric target and a date.','What do you do first in a review?','Find the top cause of errors in the data.')])
S('SG-07','Laundry and Facility Care Services','Set up a small, safe laundry or cleaning service and measure quality, turnaround and cost. You learn fabric care, chemical safety, process steps, stain handling, quality checks and costing.',
 'Run a small laundry or cleaning service for 30 days with quality checks, a turnaround record and cost per item, then report redo rate and complaints.','About 110 hours over 3 to 5 months. Partner premises with machines, a free spreadsheet, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Process sheet; safety plan; quality checks; turnaround log; cost per item; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain fabric care and cleaning basics.','Run washing or cleaning batches.','Analyse quality and cost.','Create a clear service sheet.'),
 ['Laundry attendant','Facility cleaner','Housekeeping assistant','Service owner'],
 [M('Care and fibres','Understand',8,'Different fabrics need different care. Care labels show the safe temperature and cycle. Reading them avoids damage and unhappy customers.','Learn the common fibres such as cotton, wool and synthetics, and how each reacts to heat and water.|Read the care label on every item and group items by the temperature and cycle they allow.|Separate dark and light colours to avoid dye running onto other items.|Record any item with a missing or unreadable label and ask the owner before washing it.|A common mistake is to wash a mixed load at the hottest setting to save time.','A wool jumper is 50 cm wide across the chest. A hot wash shrinks it by 8%, which is 50 times 0.08, or 4 cm, so it ends at 46 cm and no longer fits.','Sort 20 items by fibre, temperature and colour, and record the cycle you would choose for each group and why.','Your groups match the care labels for at least 19 of the 20 items and you give a reason for each cycle.','Why read care labels?','To choose the safe temperature and cycle and avoid damage.'),
  M('Safety and chemicals','Apply',14,'Cleaning products are chemicals and can harm skin, eyes and lungs. You store them properly, wear protection and never mix them. A written plan keeps everyone safe.','Keep every product in its original container with its label, stored away from food and out of reach of children.|Wear gloves and eye protection and keep the room ventilated when you handle strong products.|Never mix cleaning products, because some combinations give off toxic gas.|Measure concentrates with a marked cup and write the dilution on the container.|Check with your local authority about the rules for storing and disposing of cleaning chemicals.','A concentrate is diluted 50 mL into a 1 L total solution, so the strength is 50 divided by 1,000, which is 5%. Marking this on the bottle stops others from guessing and mixing wrongly.','Write a one-page chemical safety plan covering storage, protection, dilution, spills and disposal, and have your supervisor review it.','Your plan covers all five areas, lists each product used and is signed by the supervisor.','Why must you never mix cleaning products?','Some mixtures produce toxic gases that can cause serious harm.'),
  M('Process steps','Apply',14,'A good process makes sure every item comes back to its owner clean and undamaged. You follow the same steps every time. Tags and checks stop items getting lost.','Follow the sequence of receive, tag, check pockets, wash, dry, press and pack for every order.|Tag each order with a number that links to the customer record and not to the customer name.|Check pockets and note any damage or stains with the customer when you receive the item.|Inspect each item at the end against the checklist before it is packed.|A common mistake is to skip the pocket check, which can ruin a load.','You run 10 orders with 4 to 8 items each and tag every one. All 10 orders are returned with the right number of items, so there are 0 lost items and 0 mix-ups.','Run at least 10 orders through your process and keep a record for each with the tag number, the items in and the items out.','No items are lost or mixed up across the 10 orders, and your records match the items received and returned.','Why tag each order?','To stop mix-ups and keep every item linked to its owner.'),
  M('Stain handling','Apply',12,'Treating a stain without knowing what it is can set it forever. You identify the stain, test the method on a hidden spot and record the result. Patience beats rushing.','Ask the owner or inspect the stain to identify it, such as oil, ink, food or blood.|Choose a method that suits both the stain and the fabric, and check the care label first.|Test the method on a hidden area, for example an inside seam, before treating the visible stain.|Record the stain, the method, the result and the time taken for every treatment.|A common mistake is to use hot water on a protein stain, which can set it.','You treat 3 stains and 2 come out fully, while 1 fades but is still visible. Your success is 2 of 3, or about 67%, and the third is explained to the owner.','Treat at least three stains on practice cloth or approved items and keep a record with method and result for each.','Each record names the stain and method, the hidden-area test was done first and the result is described honestly.','Why test a method on a hidden area first?','To avoid damaging the visible part of the item.'),
  M('Quality checks','Analyse',12,'Quality means the item comes back clean, dry and neatly folded. A checklist and photos give evidence. The redo rate shows how well your process works.','Check each item against a list that includes clean, dry, no stains, no damage and neatly folded.|Take a photo of any problem so you can show the cause and the fix.|Record each redo, which is an item that needs washing again, with the reason.|Calculate the redo rate as the redos divided by the items checked.|A common mistake is to pass items quickly without checking, which moves the problem to the customer.','From 30 items checked, 1 needs a redo. The redo rate is 1 divided by 30, or about 3.3%, and the cause was a stain that was not seen before washing.','Check at least 30 finished items against your checklist and report the redo rate with causes.','Your checklist is used on each item, your redo rate is correct and every redo has a recorded cause.','What is the redo rate when 1 in 30 items needs rewashing?','1 divided by 30 is about 3.3%.'),
  M('Costs','Evaluate',14,'Knowing your cost helps you set a price that works. You include water, energy, chemicals and your time. Cost per kilogram and cost per item are the key numbers.','Record the water, energy and chemicals used for a sample of loads and put a money value on each.|Add the cost of your time and any machine wear.|Calculate cost per kg as the total cost of a load divided by its weight in kg.|Convert to cost per item using the average item weight and compare with your price.|A common mistake is to leave out your own time, which makes the service look cheaper than it is.','A load of 20 kg costs S$22 in water, energy, chemicals and time. Cost per kg is 22 divided by 20, which is S$1.10. An item of 0.5 kg costs 0.5 times 1.10, which is S$0.55.','Complete a cost sheet using at least three real loads, with cost per kg and cost per item.','Your cost per kg is correct to the nearest cent and each cost line has a source or measurement.','What is the cost per kg for a load of 20 kg that costs S$22?','22 divided by 20 is S$1.10 per kg.'),
  M('30-day run','Apply',30,'You now run the service for 30 days. Keep records for each order. The turnaround and complaint numbers tell you how well it works.','Log the date and time each order arrives and the date and time it is ready.|Calculate turnaround as the ready time minus the arrival time for each order.|Report the median turnaround, which is the middle value when the times are put in order.|Record every complaint with the cause and what you did about it.|A common mistake is to report only the best turnaround times.','Five orders take 18, 24, 24, 30 and 48 hours. In order the middle value is 24, so the median is 24 hours. The mean would be 144 divided by 5, which is 28.8 hours, and the one slow order pulls it up.','Run the service for 30 days and submit the log of items in and out, turnaround times, the median, and the complaint list.','Your log has all orders, the median is calculated correctly and every complaint has a cause.','What is the median?','The middle value when the numbers are placed in order.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',6,'The review uses your records to choose one or two improvements. You check results again after making them. Share what you learn.','Find the most common cause of redos or complaints from your logs.|Choose a fix that deals with that cause, such as sorting items before washing.|Set a target such as halving the redo rate and a date to check.|Share your findings with your mentor and peer circle so they can learn too.|A common mistake is to stop after a plan without repeating the measurement.','6 of 200 items needed a redo, which is 3%. A pre-sort step should catch most of the stain problems, so you aim to halve the rate to 1.5%, which is 3 redos in 200.','Present a short review with the top cause, your fix, your target and a date to re-measure.','Your review names one top cause with data, a specific fix, a numeric target and a date.','What do you do after your review?','Fix the top cause and measure again.')])
S('SG-08','Electronics and Device Repair','Diagnose and repair simple electronic devices safely, and record faults, parts and cost. You learn basic circuits, safe working, measuring with a multimeter, step-by-step diagnosis, careful soldering, testing and costing.',
 'Diagnose and repair five simple low-voltage devices safely and keep a repair log with test readings and cost per repair.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. Low-voltage benches, a multimeter, hand tools, a supervisor, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Safety plan signed; five repair records; test readings; cost sheet; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain voltage, current and a simple circuit.','Measure and diagnose a fault.','Analyse repeat faults.','Create a repair record.'),
 ['Repair technician assistant','Electronics bench assistant','Quality tester','Parts coordinator'],
 [M('Circuit basics','Understand',14,'A circuit is a closed loop that lets current flow. Voltage pushes the current, and resistance limits it. Knowing the link between them lets you predict what should happen.','Voltage is the push, measured in volts, current is the flow, measured in amps, and resistance is the opposition, measured in ohms.|The basic rule is that voltage equals current times resistance, written V = I x R, so current equals voltage divided by resistance.|In a series circuit the parts share one path and resistances add, while in a parallel circuit each part has its own path.|Power in watts equals volts times amps, which tells you how hot a part may get.|Work only with safe low voltage, and never practise on mains-powered equipment.','A 5 V supply across a 100 ohm resistor gives 5 divided by 100, which is 0.05 A, or 50 mA. Adding a 220 ohm resistor in series makes the total 320 ohms, and the current falls to 5 divided by 320, about 0.016 A.','Solve at least six circuit problems on paper, including two series and one parallel, and show the working and units for each.','At least five of your six answers are correct with units, and you can explain how the series and parallel results differ.','What is the current for 5 V across 100 ohms?','5 divided by 100 gives 0.05 A.'),
  M('Safety','Apply',14,'Safe habits protect you and the device. You unplug, discharge stored energy and protect parts from static. A supervisor signs off before you start.','Never open or repair a mains-powered device, and check with your local authority and your supervisor for the rules that apply.|Disconnect the power and remove any battery before you open a device, and wait for stored charge to fall.|Wear an anti-static wrist strap and work on a mat so static, also called electrostatic discharge or ESD, does not damage sensitive parts.|Keep your bench tidy, wear eye protection when you solder or cut and keep long hair and loose clothes away.|A common mistake is to assume a device is dead because it is switched off.','A 5 V device that draws 2 A uses 5 times 2, which is 10 W of power. That is enough to heat parts noticeably, so you let it cool before you touch the board.','Write and sign a bench safety plan listing at least ten rules, then have your supervisor check and sign it.','Your plan lists at least ten rules, covers power, static, heat and eye protection, and carries your supervisor signature.','What does ESD stand for and why does it matter?','It stands for electrostatic discharge, and static can silently damage sensitive electronic parts.'),
  M('Tools and measuring','Apply',16,'A multimeter measures voltage, resistance and continuity. Using it correctly gives you facts about the fault. You record every reading with its unit.','Set the dial to the right mode and range before you touch the probes to the circuit.|Measure voltage with the probes across a part and resistance only with the power off.|Use continuity mode to test whether a wire, fuse or track conducts, because it beeps when it does.|Compare each reading with the expected value and the tolerance and note whether it is inside the range.|A common mistake is to measure resistance in a live circuit, which gives false readings and can damage the meter.','A 220 ohm resistor with a 5% tolerance should read between 209 and 231 ohms, because 5% of 220 is 11. A reading of 218 ohms is inside the range, so the resistor is good.','Measure at least eight parts, including resistors, a fuse and a cable, and complete a table of expected value, tolerance range, reading and verdict.','Your table lists the unit for every reading, the tolerance ranges are calculated correctly and your verdicts match the numbers.','What does a continuity test show?','It shows whether a complete conducting path exists between two points.'),
  M('Diagnose','Analyse',20,'Diagnosis moves from symptom to cause in a logical order. You ask questions, check the simple things and isolate the fault step by step. Writing it down prevents going in circles.','Ask the user what happened, when it started and what was done before the fault appeared.|Look first at the obvious things such as the power lead, the fuse, the connectors and the battery.|Measure the voltage at the input and then at each stage, to find where it stops.|Change one thing at a time and retest, so you know which change fixed the fault.|A common mistake is to replace several parts at once, which hides the real cause and wastes parts.','A 12 V unit is dead. The input reads 0 V while the supply reads 12 V, so power does not reach the board. The fuse shows no continuity, which proves it is blown, and replacing it restores the input to 12 V.','Diagnose five faulty low-voltage devices and write a record for each with symptom, steps, readings and the confirmed cause.','Each record shows at least three measured readings, one change at a time and a cause backed by a measurement.','What is the first check on a dead unit?','Check the power supply and the fuse.'),
  M('Repair and solder','Apply',24,'Good soldering makes a clean, strong joint. You heat, feed solder and inspect. After each repair you retest to make sure the fault is gone.','Set the iron to a suitable temperature, often about 350 degrees C for common solder, and keep the tip clean and tinned.|Heat the part and the pad together for two to three seconds, then touch the solder to the joint and not to the iron.|Remove the solder first and then the iron, and let the joint cool without moving it.|Inspect each joint with a magnifier for a shiny, smooth, cone-shaped finish, and redo any dull or lumpy joint.|Use extraction or fume control at the bench and keep the area ventilated.','You make 12 joints on a test board and inspect them. Ten are shiny and cone-shaped, which is 10 divided by 12, or about 83%, and the two dull joints are reheated and fixed.','Complete at least five repairs, each with a photo of the joint, a retest reading and a note of any rework.','At least 90% of final joints pass inspection and every repair has a before and after reading.','What does a good solder joint look like?','Shiny and smooth, and shaped like a small cone.'),
  M('Test and record','Apply',16,'A repair is not finished until you have proved it works. Tests and records protect you and the customer. A soak test shows faults that appear after warm-up.','Write a short test list for each type of device that covers power, function and any safety item.|Run the device for a soak test, such as one hour, and check it at the start, the middle and the end.|Record the readings and the result of each test with the date and your initials.|Keep a return record so repeat faults can be compared.|A common mistake is to hand a device back after a one-minute test.','You soak test five repaired devices for one hour. Four pass and one stops at 40 minutes. That is 4 divided by 5, or 80%, passing, and the one that failed goes back for further diagnosis.','Complete a test and record sheet for each of your five repairs, with the soak test results and sign-off.','All five repairs have a full record, the soak test result is recorded and any failures are followed up.','Why do you run a soak test?','It finds faults that appear only after the device has warmed up.'),
  M('Cost','Evaluate',12,'Each repair has a cost made of parts, time and risk. You work it out so you can price fairly. A warranty is part of the price.','List the parts used with their cost and the time spent on the repair.|Value your time at a stated hourly rate and include a small amount for tools and consumables.|Add the cost of parts and time to get the cost per repair, and add a margin to set the price.|Decide the warranty period and the cost you set aside for rework.|A common mistake is to leave out your time or the cost of a repeat repair.','A repair uses S$6 of parts and half an hour of time valued at S$16 an hour, which is S$8. The cost per repair is 6 plus 8, which is S$14.','Complete a cost sheet for all five repairs with parts, time, total cost and proposed price.','Your totals are correct, every cost has a note and your warranty reasoning is written down.','Why offer a warranty?','It builds trust and shows that you stand behind the work.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',24,'The review looks across all your repairs for patterns. Repeat faults point to a weakness in the design or the way devices are used. You write down what you would do differently.','Group your five faults by type, such as cable break, blown fuse or bad joint.|Count how often each type occurs and note which parts fail most.|Suggest a prevention for the most common fault, such as strain relief on a cable.|Review your safety record and note any near misses and how to avoid them.|A common mistake is to review only the success stories.','Out of 5 repairs, 2 were broken charging cables at the plug, which is 2 divided by 5, or 40%. Adding strain relief or advising the user to hold the plug would prevent most of these.','Present a review with a faults table, one prevention idea and a plan for your next five repairs.','Your table totals match your records, and your prevention idea links to the most common fault.','What do repeat faults tell you?','They show a weakness in design or use that you can fix or advise on.')],
 safety="Learning only. Work only on low-voltage devices under supervision. Never open mains-powered devices without a qualified person, and check with your local authority for the rules that apply to you.")
S('SG-09','Urban and Vertical Agriculture','Grow food in small urban spaces using water and space wisely, and record yield and cost. You learn what plants need, how to choose crops, build a system, keep daily care records, measure yield, work out cost per kilo and sell safely.',
 'Run a 45-day small urban growing system and report yield per square metre, water use per kilo and cost per kilo, with a plan to scale or stop.','About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. A balcony, rooftop or indoor rack, containers, a free spreadsheet, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'System plan; 45-day log; yield and water numbers; cost per kilo; pest plan; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain light, water and nutrients.','Run a small system.','Analyse yield and water.','Create a plan for a bigger space.'),
 ['Urban grower','Greenhouse assistant','Community garden coordinator','Agri-tech assistant'],
 [M('Light, water, nutrients','Understand',10,'Plants need light, water and nutrients in the right balance. Too little or too much of any one causes poor growth. Measuring each lets you improve them.','Light is measured in hours per day, and leaf crops such as lettuce usually do well with about 12 hours.|Water should reach the roots without pooling, and you track the litres used each day.|Nutrients are fed in a balanced mix, and the strength is checked with a simple meter or test.|Indoor lights use power, so a 30 W light on for 12 hours uses 0.36 kWh each day.|A common mistake is to add more nutrients when plants look poor, when the real cause is too little light.','A 30 W light runs 12 hours a day, which is 30 times 12, or 360 watt-hours, or 0.36 kWh. Over 45 days that is 0.36 times 45, which is 16.2 kWh.','Survey your growing space and record the light hours, the space size in square metres and the water source, then estimate the power use if you add a light.','Your survey has measurements and units, and your power estimate is calculated correctly.','Name the three things plants need.','Light, water and nutrients.'),
  M('Choose crops','Create',10,'The best crops for a small space are fast, compact and in demand. You match the crop to your light, your season and your buyers. Fit the growing time to your 45-day run.','Prefer leaf crops and herbs, because they grow quickly and need less depth for roots.|Check the days to harvest on the seed packet and make sure it fits your 45-day window.|Choose crops that suit your light and temperature, and avoid ones that need a lot of space or support.|Ask two possible buyers which crops they would actually buy.|A common mistake is to pick a crop because it is fashionable and not because it fits the space.','Basil takes about 28 days to harvest and lettuce about 35 days. Both fit inside a 45-day run, with 17 and 10 days to spare, so you can plan one full cycle of either.','Pick two crops, write the days to harvest, the space each needs and your reasons, and ask two buyers for their view.','Your two crops both fit the growing window and have at least one reason each supported by a buyer comment or a measurement.','Why choose leaf crops for a small space?','They grow quickly and take little room.'),
  M('Build the system','Apply',20,'The system holds the plants, water and light. Good design saves space and water, and stops problems such as rot. You build it safely and document it.','Choose containers or racks that are stable, food-safe and the right depth for your crop.|Include drainage holes and a tray so water drains away from the roots and can be reused.|Place lights at the right height and on a timer, and keep electrical parts dry and away from water.|Photograph and sketch the system and label the parts with their sizes.|Check with your local authority about rules for balconies, rooftops and electrical work.','A rack has 3 tiers of 0.5 square metres each, so it has 3 times 0.5, which is 1.5 square metres of growing area. It takes up only 0.5 square metres of floor.','Build your system, test it with plain water for a day, and submit photos, a sketch and a list of parts with sizes.','Your system holds water without leaks, drains properly and has a sketch with labelled measurements.','Why does a container need drainage?','It stops water pooling, which would rot the roots.'),
  M('Daily care','Apply',24,'Plants need regular care. A simple routine for water, feeding and pest checks prevents most problems. A daily log gives you the data for later.','Water on a schedule and record the litres used each day.|Check the pH of the nutrient mix each week and keep it in the range for the crop, often about 5.8 to 6.5.|Look at the leaves for spots, holes or insects every day, and remove affected leaves.|Write the date, litres, pH and any problem in the daily log.|A common mistake is to water by habit and not by checking the plant and the mix.','A weekly check reads pH 7.2, which is above the 5.8 to 6.5 range. You add a small, measured amount of pH-down solution and retest, until the reading is 6.2, and record the change.','Run your system for 45 days and submit the daily log with water, pH checks and any pest or problem notes.','Your log is complete for at least 40 of the 45 days and any out-of-range reading shows a recorded action.','Why check pH?','It affects how well plants take up nutrients.'),
  M('Measure yield','Analyse',16,'Yield is how much food you get from the space you use. You weigh the harvest and compare it with the area and the water used. These numbers tell you if the system is good.','Weigh each harvest on a kitchen scale and record the kilograms and date.|Calculate yield per square metre as the total weight divided by the growing area.|Calculate water per kilo as the total litres used divided by the total kilograms harvested.|Compare your numbers with another crop or with an earlier harvest to see changes.|A common mistake is to weigh only the best plants.','A harvest of 9 kg from 0.5 square metres gives 9 divided by 0.5, which is 18 kg per square metre. If you used 45 litres of water for 9 kg, water per kilo is 5 litres.','Weigh all harvests, calculate yield per square metre and water per kilo, and present them in a table with one chart.','Your calculations are correct with units, and the chart shows the totals clearly.','What is the yield for 9 kg from 0.5 square metres?','9 divided by 0.5 is 18 kg per square metre.'),
  M('Cost per kilo','Evaluate',16,'To know whether growing pays you must add up every cost. Indoor systems use power, so include it. The cost per kilo is the key number.','List the cost of seeds, nutrients, power, containers share and your time.|Spread the cost of long-lasting items, such as racks, over the harvests you expect.|Add all costs and divide by the kilograms harvested to get the cost per kilo.|Compare your cost per kilo with the price you can sell at to find the margin.|A common mistake is to leave out power and labour.','Seeds cost S$5, nutrients S$7, power S$10, the share of the container S$8 and your time S$6, so the total is S$36. With 8 kg harvested, the cost per kilo is 36 divided by 8, which is S$4.50.','Complete a cost sheet showing every cost, total, kilograms and cost per kilo.','Your cost per kilo is correct and each cost line has a note, receipt or measurement behind it.','Why include power in the cost?','Indoor systems use costly power, and leaving it out hides the true cost.'),
  M('Sell and share','Apply',14,'Fresh produce sells best when it is clean, cool and local. You handle it hygienically and keep records. Sharing with neighbours builds community.','Harvest in the cool of the day, wash with clean water and dry the produce gently.|Pack in clean containers, keep it cool and label it with the name, the date and your contact.|Sell close by, such as to neighbours or a nearby shop, to keep it fresh and the transport short.|Record each sale with the date, weight and price, and keep a small amount to share.|Check with your local authority about the rules for selling or giving away food.','You sell 8 kg at S$8 per kilo, so you earn S$64. With a cost of S$4.50 per kilo, the margin per kilo is S$3.50, which is 3.50 divided by 8, or about 44%.','Sell or share your produce, keep a record of each sale and calculate your margin per kilo.','Your sales record is complete, your margin is calculated correctly and the food was handled and labelled as your local authority requires.','Why sell close to where you grow?','The produce stays fresher and transport costs and travel are low.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',10,'The review decides whether to scale up, change or stop. Use your numbers on yield, cost and demand. Write the decision down with reasons.','Compare yield, water use and cost with your targets and with the earlier numbers.|Note the main lessons, such as what plants did best and what problems recurred.|Estimate the benefit and cost of adding more space, using your cost per kilo and margin.|Decide to scale, change or stop and set a date for the next step.|A common mistake is to scale up before the numbers show it will pay.','A second rack costs S$60 and could add 8 kg a cycle. At a margin of S$3.50 per kilo that is 8 times 3.50, or S$28 per cycle, so it pays back in 60 divided by 28, which is 2.1, so three cycles.','Present a review with your results table, lessons and a scale, change or stop decision with reasons.','Your decision is supported by at least three numbers from your records and includes a date for the next step.','What decides whether to scale up?','Yield, cost and demand.')])
S('SG-10','Biotechnology Laboratory Skills','Learn safe laboratory practice, record keeping and simple measurement, using approved teaching activities. You learn safety, notebooks, weighing and pipetting, solutions, clean technique, data and error, ethics and reporting.',
 'Complete six supervised teaching-lab exercises with accurate records, safe handling and a final data report that includes a table, a chart and the limits of your results.','About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. A supervised teaching lab or simulation, a free spreadsheet, a supervisor, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Safety sign-off; six records; accurate pipetting and weighing; data report; at least 70% on the final check.',
 O4('Explain why records and safety matter in a lab.','Measure, pipette and prepare solutions.','Analyse data and errors.','Write a clear data report.'),
 ['Laboratory assistant','Quality control assistant','Sample handler','Research assistant'],
 [M('Lab safety','Apply',14,'Safe habits come first in a laboratory. You wear protection, know the exits and handle waste properly. A signed induction shows you are ready to start.','Wear a lab coat, gloves and eye protection at all times in the work area, and tie back long hair.|Learn the location of exits, the first-aid kit, the eye wash and the fire equipment before you start work.|Never taste, sniff or touch chemicals or samples, and never eat or drink in the lab.|Label every container with the contents, the concentration, the date and your initials.|Put waste in the correct bin as your supervisor shows you, and check with your local authority for waste rules.','A 250 mL bottle with no label is found on the bench. It cannot be used, and goes to the supervisor. A correct label carries four items: the name, the concentration, the date and the initials.','Complete the induction, pass a short safety quiz and sign the safety agreement, with your supervisor countersigning.','You score at least 90% on the quiz, find all safety points on a walk-round and hold a signed induction record.','Why label everything?','Unlabelled items cause accidents and wasted work.'),
  M('Records','Understand',12,'A lab record is the proof of what you did. If it is not written, it did not happen. Clear, honest records let others repeat your work.','Write entries in ink with the date, and record what you did at the time and not later from memory.|Correct a mistake by drawing a single line through it and adding your initials and the date, so the original is still readable.|Put units on every number, such as 5.02 g or 10.0 mL.|Include the method, the materials, the batch or lot numbers and any change from the plan.|A common mistake is to use scrap paper and copy the notes later.','You weigh a sample and write 5.02 g with the date and your initials. Later you notice a slip, so you cross it out with one line, write the correct value of 5.20 g and initial it.','Keep a lab notebook for all six exercises and have your supervisor check at least three entries.','Every entry is dated, has units, shows corrections by a single line with initials, and your supervisor can repeat the work from it.','How do you correct an error in a lab notebook?','Draw a single line through it, then add the correct value, your initials and the date.'),
  M('Measure accurately','Apply',22,'Accurate weighing and pipetting need care and practice. You check your skill by repeating measurements. Small errors early can grow into large errors later.','Level the balance, tare it with the container and handle it gently with the doors closed.|Hold the pipette upright, press to the first stop, draw up slowly by easing the plunger back, and dispense by pressing to the first stop and then on to the second stop.|Use a new tip for each sample and check the volume is in range for the pipette.|Repeat each measurement three times to see how close the results are.|A common mistake is to pipette too fast, which traps air and gives a wrong volume.','You pipette 1.00 mL of water three times and weigh each. The readings are 0.99 g, 1.01 g and 1.00 g, so the mean is 1.00 g and the range is 0.02 g, which is within 2% of the target.','Complete a pipetting and weighing exercise with at least nine repeat measurements and calculate the mean and range for each volume.','Your mean is within 2% of the target for each volume and your range is recorded correctly.','Why repeat measurements?','To check precision, meaning how close the results are to each other.'),
  M('Solutions','Apply',22,'A solution is a substance dissolved in a liquid. Concentration tells you how much is in a given volume. You calculate first, then prepare and label.','Concentration is the amount of substance divided by the volume, for example grams per litre.|Convert millilitres to litres by dividing by 1,000 before you calculate in grams per litre.|To dilute, use the rule that the starting concentration times the starting volume equals the final concentration times the final volume.|Add the solid or stock to a little liquid, mix, then top up to the final volume and label.|A common mistake is to add the full volume of water on top of the stock, which gives a final volume that is too large.','5 g dissolved to make 100 mL is 5 divided by 0.1 L, which is 50 g/L. To make 100 mL at 10 g/L from this stock, you need 10 times 100 divided by 50, which is 20 mL of stock topped up with 80 mL of water.','Prepare three solutions of different concentrations, with the calculation shown, and label each one correctly.','Your calculations are correct, your prepared solutions match the target within the tolerance and each is fully labelled.','What is the concentration of 5 g in 100 mL?','5 g divided by 0.1 L gives 50 g/L.'),
  M('Sterile technique','Apply',20,'Sterile technique keeps unwanted organisms out of your work. Small habits such as clean surfaces and quick, careful handling make a big difference. You also dispose of waste correctly.','Wipe the bench with the approved disinfectant before and after work, and wash your hands.|Work close to the flame or in the hood only as your supervisor has taught, and keep lids off for as short a time as possible.|Do not touch the inside of lids or the parts of tools that touch samples.|Put used items in the right waste container, and treat waste as your supervisor instructs, for example by autoclaving at the setting they give.|A common mistake is to talk or lean over open containers.','You prepare 10 plates and 1 shows unexpected growth. That is 1 divided by 10, or 10%, so you review each step to find where contamination entered and record the likely cause.','Practise sterile technique on approved exercises while your supervisor observes and writes a note on your habits.','Your supervisor notes correct habits on all steps and your contamination rate in practice is no more than 10%.','Why use sterile technique?','It prevents contamination, which would spoil results and could be unsafe.'),
  M('Data and error','Analyse',24,'Data has a centre and a spread. The mean describes the centre and the range describes the spread. Looking for outliers and sources of error helps you trust your results.','The mean is the sum of the values divided by how many there are.|The range is the largest value minus the smallest value.|An outlier is a value far from the rest, and you should look for a cause before you decide how to treat it.|List the sources of error, such as reading a scale, a worn pipette tip or a temperature change.|A common mistake is to report only the mean and not the spread.','Three readings are 9.6, 9.8 and 10.0. The sum is 29.4, so the mean is 29.4 divided by 3, which is 9.8. The range is 10.0 minus 9.6, which is 0.4.','Analyse the results from at least three exercises by calculating the mean and range and writing the likely sources of error.','Your means and ranges are correct and you list at least two realistic sources of error for each exercise.','What is the mean of 9.6, 9.8 and 10.0?','The sum is 29.4, and divided by 3 the mean is 9.8.'),
  M('Ethics and quality','Evaluate',16,'Honest work matters more than neat results. You never change data and you follow the rules on consent and the care of living things. Methods must be clear enough for someone else to repeat.','Never change, invent or delete a result to make it look better, and report all values including odd ones.|Follow the rules on consent, privacy and the treatment of any living things, and check with your local authority and your supervisor.|Write the method clearly enough that another person could repeat it and get similar results.|Report anything that went wrong, such as a spill or a mistake, so others can learn.|A common mistake is to drop an outlier without telling anyone.','Five readings are 9.8, 9.9, 9.7, 9.8 and 11.5. All five have a mean of 50.7 divided by 5, which is 10.14. Without the outlier the mean is 39.2 divided by 4, which is 9.8. You report both and explain the choice.','Write a one-page note on how you kept honest records, how you handled one outlier and how you would make a method repeatable.','Your note reports both values with and without any outlier and explains your reasoning, and your supervisor agrees it is honest and clear.','Why must you never alter data?','It breaks trust and can lead to harm if others rely on it.'),
  M('Report','Create',20,'A short, clear report shares your findings with others. It states the aim, method, results and limits in plain words. A table and a chart make the numbers easy to see.','Open with a title and a one-sentence aim that says what you set out to find.|Describe the method briefly with the key materials and steps, so a reader can repeat it.|Present the results in a table with units and one clear chart with labelled axes.|State one conclusion supported by the numbers and list the limits, such as the small number of repeats.|A common mistake is to draw a conclusion that your data does not support.','Your report has a title, an aim, a method of five lines, a table of six exercises, one chart of means with ranges and a conclusion that the pipetting stayed within 2% of target, with a limit that only three repeats were used.','Write a two-page report on your six exercises and give it to a peer to review, then revise it based on their notes.','Your report has all required parts, a peer confirms it is clear and your conclusion matches the data in your table.','What goes into a lab report?','The aim, the method, the results and the limits.')],
 safety="Learning only. Work only in an approved, supervised teaching setting. Do not culture or handle unknown biological material outside it, and check with your local authority for the rules that apply to you.")
